# ===========================================================================
# CloudPot - Cowrie credential database
#
# Copy to etc/userdb.txt on the sensor. Format is one rule per line:
#
#   username : uid : password
#
# with two special forms:
#   *           matches anything
#   !value      explicitly DENIES that value
#
# Rules are evaluated top to bottom; the first match wins.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY WEAK CREDENTIALS ARE THE CORRECT CHOICE HERE
#
# The instinct is to make the honeypot hard to get into. That instinct is
# backwards for this sensor. A honeypot that never accepts a login produces
# exactly one kind of data - failed authentication attempts - which tells you
# who is knocking and what they are guessing, and nothing at all about what
# they intended to do. Every interesting question this project asks lives on
# the far side of a successful login: what commands run first, what
# persistence is attempted, which URLs the payload stage reaches for, how long
# the operator stays, whether it is a human or a script.
#
# So the credential set is chosen to match what mass SSH brute-force actually
# sprays. These pairs come from the top of every public credential-stuffing
# list; a bot working its dictionary hits one in its first few dozen attempts,
# authenticates, and proceeds to the behaviour we want to observe. The weak
# credentials are the aperture, not a weakness.
#
# The obvious alternative is a single wildcard line - accept every password
# for every user. It is rejected here for two reasons. First, some botnets
# validate: they retry with a deliberately absurd password, and a host that
# accepts "asdkjhasd8712" as root is flagged as a honeypot and abandoned.
# Second, accepting everything destroys the credential-frequency analysis -
# every guess becomes a success and you lose the ability to say which
# passwords the population is actually converging on.
#
# There is no risk in publishing this list: it grants access to an emulated
# shell on a disposable host, and the real sshd is on another port behind a
# single-address security group.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Explicit denials, evaluated first -------------------------------------
# Refuse the handful of pairs that are so obviously bait that accepting them
# is itself a honeypot tell, and refuse the sensor's own admin username so a
# lucky guess never produces a session that looks like operator activity in
# the data.
cloudpot:x:!*
honeypot:x:!*

# --- root ------------------------------------------------------------------
# root is the overwhelming majority of attempts against port 22. These are the
# passwords that dominate every public brute-force dataset.
root:x:123456
root:x:root
root:x:toor
root:x:password
root:x:admin
root:x:1234
root:x:12345
root:x:1qaz@WSX
root:x:P@ssw0rd
root:x:qwerty
root:x:changeme
root:x:raspberry

# --- admin / generic service accounts --------------------------------------
admin:x:admin
admin:x:admin123
admin:x:password
admin:x:1234

# --- distribution default accounts ------------------------------------------
# ubuntu/ubuntu is specifically worth accepting: it is what a bot expects to
# work on a misconfigured cloud image, which is exactly the host this sensor
# is pretending to be.
ubuntu:x:ubuntu
ubuntu:x:ubuntu123
debian:x:debian
centos:x:centos

# --- application and appliance accounts -------------------------------------
# These distinguish targeted scanning from generic spray. A bot that tries
# oracle, postgres or git is running a list aimed at servers, not at consumer
# routers, and that distinction shows up clearly in the credential analysis.
user:x:user
test:x:test
test:x:123456
oracle:x:oracle
postgres:x:postgres
mysql:x:mysql
git:x:git
deploy:x:deploy
jenkins:x:jenkins
docker:x:docker
pi:x:raspberry
ftp:x:ftp
guest:x:guest
support:x:support

# --- default deny ----------------------------------------------------------
# Anything not listed above fails authentication and is logged as
# cowrie.login.failed. This line is what preserves the success/failure ratio
# that makes credential-frequency analysis meaningful. Do not replace it with
# a wildcard accept without re-reading the rationale at the top of this file.
*:x:!*
